frac{n}{2}){a}^{n-2}{b}^{2}+…+(\\frac{n}{n}){a}^{0}{b}^{n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x+7y)}^{10},$ we have $a=x,$ $b=7y,$ and $n=10.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x+7y)}^{10}=(\\frac{10}{0}){x}^{10}(7y)^{0}+(\\frac{10}{1}){x}^{10-1}(7y)^{1}+(\\frac{10}{2}){x}^{10-2}(7y)^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{10}{10}){x}^{10-10}(7y)^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the ${x}^{7}{y}^{3}$ term must be\n\n$$\n(\\frac{10}{3}){x}^{10-3}(7y)^{3}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying this term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{10}{3}){x}^{10-3}(7y)^{3} & =120{x}^{7}\\cdot {7}^{3}{y}^{3} \\\\  & =120{x}^{7}\\cdot 343{y}^{3} \\\\  & =41\\,160{x}^{7}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of ${x}^{7}{y}^{3}$ is $41\\,160.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-18625/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8de0dd63-23c6-5926-9e33-71e517949af2"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-18625"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5153"] :question/problem "Expand ${( 3x + y )}^3$ using the binomial theorem." :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(3x +y)}^3,$ we have  $a=3x,$ $b=y,$ and $n=3.$ \n\nTherefore, we can expand ${(3x + y)}^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x+y)^{3} & =(\\frac{3}{0})(3x)^{3}(y)^{0}+(\\frac{3}{1})(3x)^{3-1}(y)^{1}+(\\frac{3}{2})(3x)^{3-2}(y)^{2}+(\\frac{3}{3})(3x)^{3-3}(y)^{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{0})(3x)^{3}(1)+(\\frac{3}{1})(3x)^{2}(y)^{1}+(\\frac{3}{2})(3x)^{1}(y)^{2}+(\\frac{3}{3})(3x)^{0}(y)^{3} \\\\  & =1\\cdot (3x)^{3}+3\\cdot (3x)^{2}(y)+3\\cdot (3x)(y)^{2}+1\\cdot 1\\cdot ({y}^{3}) \\\\  & =27{x}^{3}+27{x}^{2}y+9x{y}^{2}+{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9775"] :question/problem "Find the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${( x - 3y )}^ 5.$" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5,$ we have  $a=x,$ $b=-3y ,$ and $n=5.$ \n\nTherefore, the first three terms of ${(x - 3y)}^ 5$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3y)^{5} & =(\\frac{5}{0})(x)^{5}(-3y)^{0}+(\\frac{5}{1})(x)^{5-1}(-3y)^{1}+(\\frac{5}{2})(x)^{5-2}(-3y)^{2}+⋯ \\\\  & =1(x)^{5}+5(x)^{4}(-3y)+10(x)^{3}(9{y}^{2})+⋯ \\\\  & ={x}^{5}-15{x}^{4}y+90{x}^{3}{y}^{2}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5154"] :question/problem "Find the coefficient of $x^{4}$ in the expansion of ${\\left( {3 + x} \\right)^{7}} .$" :question/worked-solution "The binomial theorem states that for any positive integer $n,$ we have\n\n \n\n$$\n{(a + b)^n} = {n \\choose 0}{a^n}{b^0} + {n \\choose 1}{a^{n - 1}}{b^1} + {n \\choose 2}{a^{n - 2}}{b^2} +  \\cdots  + {n \\choose n}{a^0}{b^n}.\n$$\n\nIn the case of ${\\left( {3 +x} \\right)^{7}},$ we have $a=3,$ $b=x,$ and $n=7.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3+x)^{7}=(\\frac{7}{0}){3}^{7}{x}^{0}+(\\frac{7}{1}){3}^{7-1}{x}^{1}+(\\frac{7}{2}){3}^{7-2}{x}^{2}+⋯+(\\frac{7}{7}){3}^{7-7}{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that the $x^4$ term must be\n\n$$\n{7 \\choose 4}{3^{7 - 4}}{x^4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\frac{7}{4}){3}^{7-4} & =35\\cdot {3}^{3} \\\\  & =35\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =945.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of $x^{4}$ is $945.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
